# Coach speech: the appraisal after the game (src/coach/appraisal.cpp).
# English is the reference language. Same syntax as assets/i18n: "key = value", '#' comments.
#
# Translators, read this first:
# - "key", "key.2", "key.3" are alternative phrasings of the same message; keep the same number.
# - ".b1" ... ".b6" at the end of a key is the coach level (1 = beginner, warm and simple; 6 =
#   expert, terse). The appraisal is always positive and never blames: keep that tone.
# - Placeholders are filled by the program; keep every placeholder of the English line (word order
#   is free). The coach points at the board on {move} when the piece is still there.
#     {moves}            full moves the game lasted
#     {move}             one of the player's moves; {n} its move number (or a count, see the line)
#     {my}               a piece of the coach's that the player captured: "my knight"
#     {n} {m} {k}        counts; "{n:move}" says "3 moves", see count.<noun> in common.lang
#     {pts}              points of material
#     {acc} {acc_coach} {acc_phase}  accuracy in percent, whole numbers
#     {text}             a name from this file: appraisal.phase.*, appraisal.reason.*
#     {theme}            a phrase from this file: theme.*
#     {crit_no}          the move number of the critical moment; {crit_move} the move played there,
#                        {crit_best} the better move
#     {eval}             an evaluation from the player's side: "+1.3", "mate in 4"
#     {opening}          the name of the opening
#     {level}            a level name: appraisal.level.*
#   Never write "your" or "my" before {my}: the possessive is already in it.

